readings.c1s1:
¶
'''accomplishable''' (ạ-kom′plish-a̤-bl), ''a.'' Capable of being accomplished.

'''accomplished''' (ạ-kom′plisht), ''p. a.'' '''1.''' Completed; effected: as, an ''accomplished'' fact.—'''2.''' Perfected; finished; consummate: used in either a good or a bad sense: as, an ''accomplished'' scholar; an ''accomplished'' villain.
{{smaller block|Know you not the Egyptian Zabdas?—the mirror of ''accomplished'' knighthood—the pillar of the state—the Aurelian of the East? {{right|''W. Ware'', Zenobia, I. 69.}}}}
'''3.''' Possessing accomplishments; having the attainments and graces of cultivated or fashionable society.
{{smaller block|An ''accomplished'' and beautiful young lady.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Newcomes.}}}}

'''accomplisher''' (ạ-kom′plish-ėr), ''n.'' One who accomplishes or fulfils.
{{smaller block|The Fates, after all, are the ''accomplishers'' of our hopes.
{{right|''Thoreau'', Letters, p. 26.}}}}

'''accomplishing''' (ạ-kom′plish-ing), ''n.'' That which is accomplished or completed. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|I shall simply enumerate, as ends, all that a university should accomplish, although these ''accomplishings'' may, strictly considered, often partake more of the character of means. {{right|''Sir W. Hamilton.''}}}}

'''accomplishment''' (ạ-kom′plish-ment), ''n.'' [< ''accomplish'' + ''-ment'', after F. ''accomplissement.''] '''1.''' The act of accomplishing or carrying into effect; fulfilment; achievement: as, the ''accomplishment'' of a prophecy; the ''accomplishment'' of our desires or ends.
{{smaller block|I once had faith and force enough to form generous}}

readings.c1s2:
{{smaller block|hopes of the world’s destiny . . . and to do what in me lay for their ''accomplishment.''
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Blithedale Romance, ii.}}}}

'''2.''' An acquirement; an attainment, especially such as belongs to cultivated or fashionable society: generally in the plural.
{{smaller block|I was then young enough, and silly enough, to think gaming was one of their ''accomplishments.''
{{right|''Chesterfield'', Letters.}}}}
{{smaller block|Yet wanting the ''accomplishment'' of verse.
{{right|''Wordsworth.''}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn. 1.''' Completion, fulfilment, perfection, performance, execution, achievement.—'''2.''' ''Acquirements, Acquisitions, Attainments'', etc. (see ''acquirement''), qualifications, skill, graces.}}

'''accompt'''†, '''accomptable'''†, '''accomptant'''†. See ''account'', etc. {{smaller|[The spellings ''accompt, accomptable'', etc., are artificial forms used, not prevailingly, in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries. They are now obsolete, or nearly so, though ''accompt'' and ''accomptant'' may still be used in the formal or legal style. The pronunciation has always conformed to the regular spelling, ''account, accountable'', etc.]}}

'''accourage'''†, ''v. t.'' See ''accourage. Spenser.''

'''accord''' (a̤-kôrd′), ''v.'' [< ME. ''acorden'' (less frequently ''accorden''), agree, be in harmony, trans. bring into agreement, < OF. ''acorder'', agree (F. ''accorder'' = Sp. Pr. Pg. ''acordar'' = It. ''accordare''), < ML. ''accordare'', agree, < L. ''ad'', to, + ''cor'' (''cord-'') = E. ''heart.'' Cf. ''concord'' and ''discord.''] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To agree; be in correspondence or harmony.
{{smaller block|My heart ''accordeth'' with my tongue.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>That mind and soul, ''according'' well,
May make one music as before.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', In Memoriam (Int.).}}}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|Their minds ''accorded'' into one strain, and made delightful music.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Snow Image, p. 58.}}}}

'''2.''' To make an agreement; come to an understanding.
{{smaller block|We ''accorded'' before dinner.
{{right|''Scott'', Waverley, II. xix.}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To make to agree or correspond; adapt, as one thing to another. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Her hands ''accorded'' the lute’s music to the voice.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Arcadia, ii.}}}}

'''2.''' To bring to an agreement or a settlement; settle, adjust, or compose; reconcile: as, to ''accord'' controversies.
{{smaller block|Hauing much a-doe to ''accord'' differing Writers, and to pick trueth out of partiality.
{{right|''Sir P. Sidney'', Apol. for Poetrie.}}
Is there no way left open to ''accord'' this difference,
But you must make one with your swords?
{{right|''Longfellow'', Spanish Student, ii. 6.}}}}

'''3.''' To grant; give; concede: as, to ''accord'' due praise to any one.
{{smaller block|His hands were thrust into his pockets; he was whistling thoughtfully, and walking to and fro, a small space having been ''accorded'' him by the crowd, in deference to his temporary importance.
{{right|''Irving'', Sketch-Book, p. 23.}}}}

'''accord''' (a-kôrd′), ''n.'' [< ME. ''acord'' (less frequently ''accord''), < OF. ''acorde'', usually ''acort'', agreement (F. ''accord'' = Sp. ''acorde'' = Pg. ''acordo, accordo''), verbal n. of ''acorder'', agree: see ''accord'', ''v.''] '''1.''' Agreement; harmony of minds; consent or concurrence of opinions or wills; assent.
{{smaller block|These all continued with one ''accord'' in prayer and supplication.
{{right|Acts i. 14.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|<poem>You must buy that peace
With full ''accord'' to all our just demands.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., v. 2.}}}}

'''2.''' A union of different sounds which is agreeable to the ear; concord; harmony.
{{smaller block|Those sweet ''accords'' are even the angels' lays.
{{right|''Sir J. Davies'', Immortal. of Soul, ii. 1.}}}}

'''3.''' Agreement; just correspondence of things; harmony of relation: as, the ''accord'' of light and shade in painting.
{{smaller block|Beauty is nothing else but a just ''accord'' and mutual harmony of the members, animated by a healthful constitution. {{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Dufresnoy’s Art of Painting, Pref.}}}}

'''4.''' Will; voluntary or spontaneous impulse or act; unaided action or operation: preceded by ''own''.
{{smaller block|Being more forward, of his ''own accord'' he went unto you. {{right|2 Cor. viii. 17.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Now of my ''own accord'' such other trial
I mean to show you of my strength.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', S. A., l. 1643.}}}}

'''5.''' Adjustment of a difference; reconciliation: as, the mediator of an ''accord''.
{{smaller block|<poem>If both are satisfied with this ''accord'',
Swear by the laws of knighthood on my sword.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Fables.}}}}
{{smaller block|Specifically, in ''law'', an agreement which is made between parties for the settlement of a liability or controversy, and which, when executed, that is, carried into effect, is termed an ''accord and satisfaction'', and bars or terminates a suit; a private extra-judicial agreement or arrangement.}}

'''6.''' In ''music'', same as ''chord''.—'''7.''' ''Milit.'', the conditions under which a fortress or command of troops is surrendered.—{{smaller|'''To be at accord''', to be in agreement. ''Chaucer''.—'''To fall of accord†''', to come into agreement. ''Chaucer''.}}

readings.c2s2:
¶
'''accordable†''' (a-kôr′da-bl), ''a.'' [< ME. ''acordable'', < OF. *''acordable'', F. ''accordable'', < OF. ''acorder'': see ''accord''. Cf. Sp. ''acordablemente'', adv.] Capable of being harmonized or reconciled; consonant; agreeable.

'''accordance''' (a-kôr′dans), ''n.'' [< ME. ''acordance'', ''acordaunce'', < OF. ''acordance'', later ''accordance'' (= Pr. ''acordansa''), < ''acordant'', etc.: see ''accordant''.] '''1.''' The state of being in accord; agreement with a person; conformity to a thing; harmony.
{{smaller block|Their voices are in admirable ''accordance'' with the tranquil solitude of a summer afternoon.
{{right|''Hawthorne'', Old Manse.}}
There is a remarkable ''accordance'' in the power of digestion between the gastric juice of animals with its pepsin and hydrochloric acid, and the secretion of ''Drosera'' with its ferment and acid belonging to the acetic series.
{{right|''Darwin'', Insectiv. Plants, vi.}}}}
'''2.''' The act of according, granting, or giving. ='''Syn. 1.''' {{smaller|Harmony, unison, coincidence.}}

'''accordancy''' (a-kôr′dan-si), ''n.'' Same as ''accordance'', but less used.

'''accordant''' (a-kôr′dant), ''a.'' [< ME. ''acordant'', ''acordaunt'', < OF. ''acordant'', F. ''accordant'', agreeing with, < ML. ''accordan(t-)s'', ppr. of ''accordare'', agree: see ''accord'', ''v.''] Corresponding; conformable; consonant; agreeable; of the same mind; harmonious: sometimes followed by ''to'', but more commonly by ''with'': as, this was not ''accordant to'' his tastes, or ''with'' his principles.
{{smaller block|If he found her ''accordant''.
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, i. 2.}}
Music and meaning floated together, ''accordant'' as swan and shadow.
{{right|''Lowell'', Among my Books, 2d ser., p. 326.}}}}

readings.c2s3:
¶
{{smaller block|<poem>In the neighboring hall a strain of music, proceeding
From the ''accordant'' strings of Michael’s melodious fiddle.</poem>
{{right|''Longfellow'', Evangeline, ii. 3.}}}}

'''accordantly''' (a-kôr′dạnt-li), ''adv.'' In an accordant manner; in accordance or agreement.

'''accorder''' (a̤-kôr′dėr), ''n.'' One who accords or agrees; one who grants or bestows. [Rare.]

'''according''' (a̤-kôr′ding), ''p. a.'' '''1.''' Agreeing; harmonious.
{{smaller block|Th' ''according'' music of a well-mixed state.
{{right|''Pope'', Essay on Man, iii. 294.}}}}
'''2.''' Suitable; agreeable; in accordance; in proportion: followed by ''to''.
{{smaller block|Our zeal should be ''according to'' knowledge. {{right|''Bp. Sprat.''}}}}

'''according''' (a̤-kôr′ding), ''adv.'' In accordance (with); agreeably (to): used with ''to'': as, he acted ''according to'' his judgment: often applied to persons, but referring elliptically to their statements or opinions. Often abbreviated to ''acc.''
{{smaller block|''According to'' him, every person was to be bought.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., i.}}
<poem>For no delicious morsel pass’d her throat;
''According to'' her cloth she cut her coat.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Cock and Fox, l. 20.}}}}

{{smaller|'''According as''', agreeably, conformably, or proportionately as.}}
{{smaller block|A man may, with prudence and a good conscience, approve of the professed principles of one party more than the other, ''according as'' he thinks they best promote the good of church and state.
{{right|''Swift'', Sentiments of a Ch. of Eng. Man, i.}}}}

readings.c3s1:
¶
'''accordingly''' (a̤-kôr′ding-li), ''adv.'' '''1.''' Agreeably; suitably; in a manner conformable: as, those who live in faith and good works will be rewarded ''accordingly.''
{{smaller block|Whenever you are to do a thing, though it can never be known but to yourself, ask yourself how you would act were all the world looking at you, and act ''accordingly.''
{{right|''Jefferson'', Correspondence, I. 286.}}}}
'''2.''' In assent or compliance; acquiescently.
{{smaller block|Upon this the Sultan was directed to place himself by a huge tub of water; which he did ''accordingly.''
{{right|''Addison'', Spectator, No. 94.}}}}
{{smaller block|=Syn. '''2.''' ''Therefore'', ''Wherefore'', ''Accordingly'', etc. See ''therefore.''}}

'''accordion''' (a̤-kôr′di-o̤n), ''n.'' [Also spelled ''accordeon'', < F. ''accordéon'', < ''accorder'', be in harmony, accord.] A small keyed wind-instrument, opening and shutting like a bellows, and having its tones generated by the play of wind thus produced upon metallic reeds. {{smaller|It is constructed on the same principle as the concertina and the harmonium, but is much inferior to them.}}

'''accordionist''' (a̤-kôr′di-o̤n-ist), ''n.'' A player on the accordion.

'''accorporate†''' (a̤-kôr′pọ̄-rāt), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''accorporatus'', pp. of ''accorporare'', < ''ad'', to, + ''corporare'', form into a body: see ''corporate.''] To incorporate; unite.
{{smaller block|Custom, being but a mere face, as echo is a mere voice, rests not in her unaccomplishment, until by secret inclination she ''accorporate'' herself with errour.
{{right|''Milton'', Pref. to Doct. of Divorce.}}}}

'''accorporation†''' (a̤-kôr-pọ̄-rā′sho̤n), ''n.'' Incor-

readings.c3s2:
poration.

'''accost''' (a̤-kôst′), ''v.'' [< F. ''accoster'', < OF. ''acoster'', come alongside of, approach, touch, = Sp. Pg. ''acostar'' = It. ''accostare'', < ML. ''accostare'', set one’s self alongside of, < L. ''ad'', to, + ''costa'', a rib, a side: see ''coast'', ''accoast'', and ''costal''.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1'''†. To come side by side or face to face with; draw near; approach; make up to.
{{smaller block|''Accost'' [her], Sir Andrew, ''accost.''—What’s that?—''Accost'' is, front her, board her, woo her, assail her.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., i. 3.}}}}
'''2.''' To speak to; address.
{{smaller block|With taunts the distant giant I ''accost.''
{{right|''Pope'', Odyssey, x.}}
Being shown into the common room, I was ''accosted'' by a very well-dressed gentleman. {{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, xviii.}}}}
'''3'''†. To border on; adjoin.
{{smaller block|Lapland hath since been often surrounded (so much as ''accosts'' the sea) by the English.
{{right|''Fuller'', Worthies, Derbyshire.}}}}
'''II.'''† ''intrans.'' To adjoin; be adjacent.
{{smaller block|The shores which to the sea ''accoste.''
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., V. xi. 42.}}}}

'''accost''' (a̤-kôst′), ''n.'' The act of accosting; address; salutation.
{{smaller block|He revealed himself in his ''accost.''
{{right|''Arch. Forbes'', Souvenirs of some Continents, p. 101.}}}}

'''accostable''' (a̤-kôs′ta̤-bl), ''a.'' [< F. ''accostable'', < ''accoster'', approach: see ''accost'', ''v.''] Capable of being accosted; easy of access; affable.
{{smaller block|The French are a free, debonnair, ''accostable'' people.
{{right|''Howell'', Letters, ii. 12.}}}}

